40,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears we have a significant disparity in usage; some rooms draw four times more power than others. Interestingly, a fixed seasonal rate seems to remove the anxiety of using the heating. What did you all find?","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Mio,,I found out that both the reconciliation template and the room metering at another house are way too much work! It takes forever to log everything and do the math. We can't pick something that's a total nightmare to manage!,"{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"@Mio, that's a warning we should heed. I also found that usage varies wildly—some rooms use four times more than others. Plus, the payment log shows late payments whenever the amounts fluctuate.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"The financial data confirms a high level of instability; February's bill was over three times that of August. Additionally, installing individual meters would be quite expensive, costing 34,000 yen per room plus annual maintenance fees.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Minato,,"I found that people have been paying late when the bills change, and it took a long time to agree on money when new people moved in. It seems like money makes us argue.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Victoria, the cost of meters is steep, and @Mio's findings suggest the administration would be a burden. If we want to avoid the ""coat in February"" scenario, a fixed rate seems logically superior.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Victoria"",""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Mio,,"@Benjamin, I totally agree! If it's a nightmare to calculate, we'll just end up fighting again. A fixed rate sounds way simpler. Does that mean we wouldn't have to do all that crazy math every month?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Mio, yes, it would simplify things. However, we must consider that some of us use four times more energy than others. A fixed rate is easy, but is it fair to those who use very little?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Jonathan, fairness is important, but we must weigh it against the high installation costs and the administrative burden @Mio mentioned. A system that is perfectly fair but unaffordable or exhausting is not practical.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Minato,,"I agree with Victoria. If we spend all our time arguing about who used more or doing hard math, we won't be happy. Maybe a simple way is better so we can just be friends.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Jonathan, the ""fairness"" of a fixed rate is a trade-off for psychological peace. If we obsess over every watt, we risk the tension Minato mentioned. A seasonal average provides predictability, which is its own form of fairness.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Mio,,"Exactly! If we're always worrying about who's using what, we'll never relax. I'd rather have a simple system that everyone can agree on than a ""fair"" one that makes us miserable!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I understand the appeal of simplicity, but we must be careful. If the disparity is truly four times higher for some, a flat or fixed rate might just replace one kind of resentment with another.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Jonathan, that is a risk. However, the cost of meters is prohibitive and the administrative load is too high. We must decide if the cost of ""perfect fairness"" is worth the financial and emotional strain.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Minato,,"I think we should choose the one that makes us feel the most relaxed. If we worry too much about who uses more, we might stop being kind to each other. Simple is better.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are converging on the idea that simplicity and psychological comfort outweigh absolute precision. Given the high cost of meters and the administrative burden, a seasonal fixed rate appears the most rational compromise.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Mio,,I'm totally on board with that! Let's go for the seasonal fixed rate. It sounds like the best way to keep things easy and keep us all smiling!,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I can see the logic in prioritizing peace and simplicity. While the usage gap is significant, the alternatives are either too expensive or too tedious. I will support a system that prevents resentment from festering.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. The extreme volatility of our monthly bills makes a fixed rate the most stable option for our budgets, and the alternatives are simply impractical. I support the seasonal fixed rate for the sake of our collective peace.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Minato,,I agree with everyone. I'm glad we found a way to make things easier. I'll vote for the seasonal fixed rate so we can all be happy and relaxed in our home.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
